---
// src/pages/brands/perlick-residential.astro
// Perlick Residential brand pillar (Tier 2, brand-pillar wave 2026-07-17).
// Spine = the commercial/residential split, same pattern as true-residential.astro.
//
// SOURCE LOG (facts that must not drift):
//  - OWNERSHIP: founded 1917 Milwaukee by Robert K. Perlick as R. Perlick Brass Works;
//    still family-owned, FIFTH generation in leadership; "locally owned and family-operated
//    for over a century". NOT Ali Group, no parent, no acquisition on record.
//    [perlick.com/about-us; corroborated tmj4.com "105-year-old family-owned Milwaukee
//    luxury bar appliance manufacturer"]
//    HQ 8300 W. Good Hope Rd, Milwaukee (300,000 sq ft) + Monterrey, Mexico plant.
//  - RESIDENTIAL LINES: G5 LUXE, G5 Series, Signature Series, C-Series, Project Series.
//    [store.perlick.com/residential-products]
//    Categories: undercounter, column, ice makers, mobile bar, accessories.
//    [perlick.com/residential]
//    WIDTHS: 15" / 24" / 30" ONLY. Heights: 34" standard, 32" ADA.
//    *** 18" IS A DEPTH (shallow-depth variant), NOT A WIDTH. Do not "correct" it back. ***
//    Signature prefixes HP (std depth) / HH (shallow) / HC / HA — e.g. HP24RS-4, HH24BS-4.
//    G5: G5RE24SA refrigerator, G5WR24SA/G5WR24GA wine reserve. Column: CR24 / CR30.
//    Ice: H50IM clear ice; H50/H80 series.
//  - DOES NOT MAKE: ranges, ovens, cooktops, dishwashers, laundry, full-size refrigerators.
//    Residential = undercounter/column refrigeration + ice + bar only. [perlick.com/residential]
//  - ERROR CODES: THEY EXIST — unlike La Cornue / True Residential. From the Perlick
//    Residential Use & Care Guide, Form Z2344 Rev D-08/24/2020, p.12:
//      P1 "Thermostat probe has failed"    P2 "Evaporator probe has failed"
//      HA high-temp alarm exceeded 30 min  LA low-temp alarm exceeded 30 min
//    *** The manual footnote reads verbatim: "* Applies to Signature Series models only" ***
//    C-Series dial-thermostat models have NO codes (manual gives "Dial Temperature Control").
//    Wine/dual-zone HC48RS4/HC48WS4: E1, E2, AH1, AL1, OPd. Column CR24/CR30: P1-P6,
//    L1-L6, H1-H6, r5/r6, d1. [Z2344 p.12; partstown.com perlick-refrigerator-error-codes]
//    NOT VERIFIED: a dedicated service/diagnostic mode; a G5 fault-code table. Do not invent.
//  - WARRANTY (Residential Warranty Statement rev. 7/5/2022):
//      Undercounter HP/HH/HA/HC built 1/4/21+ : 6 years parts & labour (or 75 mo from ship)
//      Ice makers H50/H80                     : 2 years parts & labour (or 27 mo)
//      Column CR                              : 3 yr parts & labour + 6 yr sealed system
//                                               + TWELVE year sealed system PARTS ONLY
//      Beverage dispensing                    : 1 year parts & labour (or 15 mo)
//      G5 LUXE                                : 6 years parts & labour + 30-day quality
//    *** Never write "12-year warranty" unqualified — it is Column, sealed system, parts
//    only, no labour. The source PDF itself contains typos ("six (3) years"). ***
//    Registration at perlick.com required to validate.
//  - SERVICE CHANNEL (the unique fact): Perlick outsources residential tech service to
//    PARTS TOWN — official address techservice@partstown.com, 844-411-8050. Customer
//    service 800-558-5592. No public authorized-servicer locator, only a dealer locator.
//    [perlick.com/warranty-and-support]
//  - RAPIDcool is a real trademark (RAPIDcool(TM)) forced-air system; variable-speed
//    compressor; G5 rated indoor AND outdoor (UL) with anti-condensation.
//    [perlick.com/residential/g5-undercounter-refrigeration]
//    NOT VERIFIED: the "chills twice as fast" line — dealer copy, not perlick.com. Omitted.
//  - DEMAND: "perlick refrigerator repair" 150/mo KD 0 CPC $4.00 (the priciest click in the
//    set); "perlick appliance repair" 150/mo KD 0; "perlick" 1,900/mo. SERP: perlick.com
//    takes #1-2, then DR 1-4 filler — the niche is empty.
//
// No fabricated repair records — same rule as the T1 pages.

import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../../components/ServiceHero.astro';
import { MAIN_PHONE } from '../../data/branches';

const phone = MAIN_PHONE;
const phoneRaw = MAIN_PHONE.replace(/\D/g, '');
const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/brands/perlick-residential/";

const title = "Perlick Residential Appliance Repair Los Angeles — Same Day";
const description = "Perlick residential undercounter, column, wine & ice repair in LA. Signature Series codes, C-Series has none. Independent Milwaukee family brand. $89 diagnostic.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is the Perlick in my bar the same as the Perlick in a restaurant?",
    a: "Same company, different line — and unlike most brands in this category, the company behind it hasn't changed hands. <strong>Perlick has been family-owned since 1917</strong>, founded in Milwaukee by Robert K. Perlick as R. Perlick Brass Works, and it's now run by the fifth generation of the family. It is <strong>not</strong> owned by Ali Group or anyone else — no parent company, no acquisition on record. That's genuinely unusual: nearly every competitor in premium undercounter refrigeration has been absorbed by a conglomerate, and several changed hands again as recently as February 2026. Perlick didn't. The commercial side builds back-bar and beverage systems for venues; the residential side builds the undercounter and column refrigeration in your home bar. Same Milwaukee plant, same build language, different product and — importantly — <strong>different service channel</strong>. Our commercial Perlick work lives on the <a href=\"/brands/perlick-commercial/\">Perlick commercial refrigeration page</a>."
  },
  {
    q: "What error code is my Perlick showing?",
    a: "Depends entirely on which line you own, and this is where most pages get Perlick wrong. <strong>Signature Series models do have fault codes.</strong> From Perlick's own residential Use &amp; Care guide: <code>P1</code> means the thermostat probe has failed, <code>P2</code> means the evaporator probe has failed, <code>HA</code> means the compartment has been above the high-temperature alarm setting for more than 30 minutes, and <code>LA</code> is the same for the low-temperature alarm. The manual carries an explicit footnote on that table: it applies to <strong>Signature Series models only</strong>. So if you have a <strong>C-Series with a dial thermostat, there are no codes at all</strong> — the manual just documents dial temperature control, and diagnosis is by symptom. Wine and dual-zone units use a different set again (<code>E1</code>, <code>E2</code>, <code>AH1</code>, <code>AL1</code>, and <code>OPd</code> for a door left open), and the CR columns use a wider table (<code>P1</code>–<code>P6</code> for sensors, <code>L</code>/<code>H</code> for min and max temp). Tell us the series and the code and we'll know what we're bringing."
  },
  {
    q: "What sizes does Perlick residential actually come in?",
    a: "Widths are <strong>15, 24 and 30 inches</strong>. That's the whole range. Heights are 34 inches standard and <strong>32 inches for the ADA units</strong>. One thing worth getting right, because it causes real confusion when people are ordering parts or measuring an opening: <strong>18 inches is a depth, not a width</strong> — Perlick makes a shallow-depth variant, and the Signature prefixes encode it (<code>HP</code> is standard depth, <code>HH</code> is shallow depth). So an \"18-inch Perlick\" is almost always someone describing a shallow-depth 24-inch unit. Product families are G5 LUXE, G5 Series, Signature Series, C-Series and Project Series, plus the CR columns at 24 and 30 inches and the H50/H80 ice makers."
  },
  {
    q: "Does Perlick make ranges or dishwashers?",
    a: "No. <strong>Perlick residential is refrigeration, ice and bar equipment</strong> — undercounter refrigerators, freezers, wine reserves, beverage centres, refrigerator and freezer drawers, beverage dispensers, mobile bars, and clear-ice makers. No ranges, no ovens, no cooktops, no dishwashers, no laundry, and no full-size refrigerators either. Perlick's whole proposition is the cabinet under the counter, not the kitchen around it. If your Perlick bar sits in a kitchen with a range in it, the range is another brand — tell us what's on its model plate when you call."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the Perlick warranty on a residential unit?",
    a: "It depends on the line, and one number gets misquoted constantly. Undercounter units in the HP / HH / HA / HC families built from 4 January 2021 carry <strong>six years parts and labour</strong> (or 75 months from shipment, whichever comes first). G5 LUXE is the same six years parts and labour. <strong>Ice makers are two years.</strong> Beverage-dispensing equipment is one year. The CR columns are the odd one: three years parts and labour, six years on the sealed system, and then a <strong>twelve-year sealed-system term that is parts only — no labour</strong>. That last figure is where \"Perlick has a 12-year warranty\" comes from, and it's wrong for every undercounter unit in the range. Registration at perlick.com is required to validate coverage. Worth a warning if you're reading the PDF yourself: the 2022 statement contains a couple of typos in the spelled-out numbers, so go by the line, not by a phrase you half-remember."
  },
  {
    q: "Are you a Perlick authorized service company?",
    a: `<strong>No, we're independent.</strong> Perlick's own residential support arrangement is worth knowing, because it surprises people: <strong>Perlick outsources residential technical service to Parts Town</strong> — the official technical-service address is on the <code>partstown.com</code> domain (844-411-8050), and Perlick customer service is 800-558-5592. There's no public authorized-servicer locator, only a dealer locator; in practice warranty work routes through your dealer or distributor. So if your unit is in warranty, start there — that's the honest advice. Where an independent earns the work is out-of-warranty units, second-owner units, and the calls where you want someone this week. We're BHGS #A49573, and the invoice has our name on it. Call ${phone} with the series and model off the plate.`
  },
  {
    q: "My Perlick isn't getting cold — what's the usual cause?",
    a: "The first thing we establish is whether the unit is actually failing or working as designed. Perlick's residential units run a <strong>variable-speed compressor</strong> and a trademarked <strong>RAPIDcool</strong> forced-air system, and the G5 line adds smart defrost — so a cabinet that sounds like it's running constantly may simply be modulating rather than cycling, which is what it's supposed to do. Beyond that, the honest list is short and mechanical: airflow blocked at the grille (these are undercounter units living in cabinetry, and the front grille is the only breathing they get), a condenser packed with dust in a bar that doesn't get vacuumed, a door seal that's stopped sealing, a failed probe — which on a Signature Series will actually tell you, as a <code>P1</code> or <code>P2</code> — or a sealed-system fault, which is where the warranty tier suddenly matters. We diagnose before quoting, and if the answer is \"nothing is wrong, that's the compressor doing its job,\" we'll say that."
  },
  {
    q: "Do you service outdoor Perlick units?",
    a: "Yes. The G5 line is designed and tested for indoor <em>and</em> outdoor use — it's UL rated for outdoor installation and carries anti-condensation technology, which matters in an LA outdoor kitchen far more than it would in a climate that isn't swinging from marine layer to 95°F in an afternoon. Outdoor undercounter work has its own page: <a href=\"/brands/perlick-outdoor-refrigerator-repair/\">Perlick outdoor refrigerator repair</a>. Bar and kegerator work — draft systems, dispensers, the beer side — is covered on <a href=\"/brands/perlick-draft-beer-system-repair/\">Perlick draft beer system repair</a>."
  }
];

const schemaJsons = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Service",
    "@id": canonical + "#service",
    "serviceType": "Perlick Residential Refrigeration Repair",
    "name": "Perlick Residential Appliance Repair, Same Day Appliance Repair",
    "url": canonical,
    "description": "Same-day Perlick residential refrigeration repair across Los Angeles, Orange, Ventura, Santa Barbara, San Bernardino, and Riverside counties. Undercounter refrigerators, freezers, wine reserves, beverage centres, refrigerator and freezer drawers, CR columns (24/30 inch) and H50/H80 clear-ice makers, in 15, 24 and 30 inch widths with 32 inch ADA variants. Signature Series carries P1/P2/HA/LA fault codes; C-Series dial models have none. Perlick is independent and family-owned in Milwaukee since 1917, now fifth generation — not part of Ali Group or any conglomerate. Independent service, not factory-authorized. $89 residential diagnostic, applied toward repair.",
    "provider": {
      "@type": "LocalBusiness",
      "address": { "@type": "PostalAddress", "addressLocality": "West Hollywood", "addressRegion": "CA", "addressCountry": "US" },
      "name": "Same Day Appliance Repair",
      "legalName": "HVAC 777 LLC",
      "url": "https://samedayappliance.repair/",
      "telephone": phone,
      "priceRange": "$$$",
      "openingHoursSpecification": [
        { "@type": "OpeningHoursSpecification", "dayOfWeek": ["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday","Saturday"], "opens": "08:00", "closes": "20:00" },
        { "@type": "OpeningHoursSpecification", "dayOfWeek": "Sunday", "opens": "00:00", "closes": "00:00" }
      ],
      "hasCredential": [
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "BHGS Registration #A49573", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "GovernmentOrganization", "name": "California Bureau of Household Goods and Services" } },
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "EPA 608 Universal Certification #1346255700410", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "GovernmentOrganization", "name": "U.S. Environmental Protection Agency" } },
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "CSLB C-20 HVAC", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "GovernmentOrganization", "name": "California Contractors State License Board" } },
        { "@type": "EducationalOccupationalCredential", "credentialCategory": "BBB Accredited Business", "recognizedBy": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Better Business Bureau" } }
      ]
    },
    "areaServed": [
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Los Angeles County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Orange County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Ventura County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Santa Barbara County" }, { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "San Bernardino County" },
      { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Riverside County" }
    ],
    "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": "Perlick", "sameAs": "https://www.perlick.com/residential" },
    "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "89.00", "priceCurrency": "USD", "description": "Residential diagnostic fee, waived when the repair is approved." }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "@id": canonical + "#faq",
    "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({ "@type": "Question", "name": f.q, "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '') } }))
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "@id": canonical + "#breadcrumb",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://samedayappliance.repair/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Brands", "item": "https://samedayappliance.repair/brands/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Perlick Residential", "item": canonical }
    ]
  }
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head-scripts">
    {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
  </Fragment>

  <ServiceHero
    title={"Perlick Residential Appliance Repair Los Angeles"}
    subtitle={"Undercounter, column, wine and ice — the home side of Milwaukee's independent bar-equipment family. $89 diagnostic, waived with the repair."}
    heroImageTitle="Perlick Residential"
  />

  <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span class="crumbs-sep">›</span>
    <a href="/brands/">Brands</a> <span class="crumbs-sep">›</span>
    <span aria-current="page">Perlick Residential</span>
  </nav>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">01, the brand</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">In a category where every competitor got bought, Perlick is still the same Milwaukee family that started it in 1917.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>That's not brand romance, it's the practical fact that shapes everything about servicing one. Robert K. Perlick founded R. Perlick Brass Works in Milwaukee in 1917. The company is still family-owned and now run by the <strong>fifth generation</strong>, still headquartered on West Good Hope Road in a 300,000-square-foot plant, with a second facility in Monterrey, Mexico. No parent company. No conglomerate. <strong>Not Ali Group</strong>, which is the one people most often assume, because Ali Group owns much of the rest of the commercial refrigeration world.</p>
        <p>Look at what happened to the neighbours and the point sharpens. <a href="/brands/u-line/">U-Line</a> and <a href="/brands/marvel/">Marvel</a> — Perlick's closest competitors in premium undercounter — were Middleby brands until February 2026, when 26North Partners bought a majority of that residential portfolio and renamed it Composition Brands. <a href="/brands/true-residential/">True Residential</a> is the Trulaske family's, and gets misattributed to Middleby constantly. Perlick just kept building in Milwaukee.</p>
        <p>What that means when your unit is warm at 8am: the parts channel doesn't route through a conglomerate's distribution network that's mid-reorganisation, and the product line hasn't been redesigned to fit somebody's portfolio strategy. It also means Perlick is small enough to have made an unusual support decision, which we'll come to.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">02, the codes</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Perlick does have fault codes — but only on some lines, and that footnote is the whole story.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>This is where Perlick differs from the other hand-built brands we service. <a href="/brands/la-cornue/">La Cornue</a> has no error-code system at all. True Residential has no alphanumeric table on the display. <strong>Perlick genuinely does</strong> — and it's in their own residential Use &amp; Care guide. But the table carries a footnote that decides whether any of it applies to you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Signature Series — codes exist</h3>
          <p>The manual's table is short and honest: <code>P1</code> — the thermostat probe has failed. <code>P2</code> — the evaporator probe has failed. <code>HA</code> — the compartment has been above the high-temperature alarm setting for more than 30 minutes. <code>LA</code> — the same, below the low-temperature alarm. That's it. Four codes, and two of them are alarms rather than faults.</p>
          <p>The footnote under the table reads, verbatim: <strong>"Applies to Signature Series models only."</strong></p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>C-Series — no codes at all</h3>
          <p>If your unit has a <strong>dial thermostat</strong>, there is no code to read and never was. The manual documents dial temperature control instead, so the cabinet is diagnosed the old way — by measurement, not by lookup. Anyone reciting a P1/P2 table at a C-Series owner is reading the wrong page of the right manual.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Wine and dual-zone — a different set</h3>
          <p>Wine and dual-zone cabinets use their own vocabulary: <code>E1</code> and <code>E2</code> for probes, <code>AH1</code> and <code>AL1</code> for max and min temperature, and <code>OPd</code> — which simply means a door has been open for more than a minute. That last one gets reported to us as a fault more often than anything else on the brand.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>CR columns — the wide table</h3>
          <p>The 24 and 30-inch CR columns run a fuller set: <code>P1</code> through <code>P6</code> across the sensors, <code>L</code> and <code>H</code> series for minimum and maximum temperature, humidity codes, and a door code. More zones, more sensors, more codes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <p>What we won't do is invent the rest. There's no published G5 fault-code table and no documented service mode in the residential guide, so we don't pretend to either — if a company hands you a comprehensive Perlick code chart, ask which manual it came from.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">03, sizes and lines</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">15, 24 and 30 inches wide. If someone says "18-inch Perlick," they mean the depth.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Worth being precise, because it decides what fits your opening and what part we bring.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Widths: 15″ / 24″ / 30″</h3>
          <p>That's the entire residential range. Heights are 34 inches standard and <strong>32 inches on the ADA units</strong>, which Perlick builds in both indoor and outdoor versions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>18″ is a depth</h3>
          <p>Perlick makes a <strong>shallow-depth</strong> variant, and the Signature prefix tells you which you have: <code>HP</code> is standard depth, <code>HH</code> is shallow. So "an 18-inch Perlick" is almost always a shallow-depth 24-inch cabinet. Getting this wrong wastes a visit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>The families</h3>
          <p><strong>G5 LUXE</strong> and <strong>G5 Series</strong> at the top, <strong>Signature Series</strong> (the HP/HH/HC/HA prefixes — HP24RS-4, HH24BS-4 and relatives), <strong>C-Series</strong>, and <strong>Project Series</strong>. G5 model numbers read differently: G5RE24SA for a refrigerator, G5WR24SA or G5WR24GA for a wine reserve.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>Columns and ice</h3>
          <p><strong>CR24 and CR30</strong> columns, and the <strong>H50IM</strong> clear-ice maker with the H50/H80 series behind it. The ice makers carry their own — much shorter — warranty, which catches people out.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>What's actually in the cabinet</h3>
          <p>Undercounter refrigerators and freezers, wine reserves, beverage centres, refrigerator and freezer drawers, beverage dispensers, mobile bars. G5 adds smart defrost, monitoring, colour-controllable LED, triple-pane glass and soft-close articulating hinges.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card">
          <h3>What Perlick doesn't make</h3>
          <p>No ranges, no ovens, no cooktops, no dishwashers, no laundry, <strong>no full-size refrigerators</strong>. The whole proposition is the cabinet under the counter. Anything else in that kitchen is another brand.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">04, warranty</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">"Perlick has a 12-year warranty" is true of exactly one line, and probably not yours.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The terms are genuinely good. They're also line-dependent, and the twelve-year figure travels much further than it should.</p>
        <p><strong>Undercounter</strong> units in the HP / HH / HA / HC families built from 4 January 2021 carry <strong>six years parts and labour</strong> — or 75 months from shipment, whichever lands first. G5 LUXE matches that six years and adds a 30-day initial-quality window. <strong>Ice makers are two years.</strong> Beverage-dispensing equipment is <strong>one year</strong>.</p>
        <p>Then the <strong>CR columns</strong>, which is where the famous number lives: three years parts and labour, six years on the sealed system, and a <strong>twelve-year sealed-system term that covers parts only — no labour</strong>. So the twelve years is real, but it is one line, one subsystem, and it doesn't pay a technician. If you have a Signature undercounter and you were told you have twelve years of cover, you were told wrong.</p>
        <p>Two practical notes. <strong>Registration at perlick.com is required</strong> to validate coverage — worth checking before you assume. And if you go read the 2022 warranty statement yourself, be aware it contains a couple of typos where the spelled-out number and the numeral disagree; go by the product line rather than by a half-remembered phrase.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">05, service channel</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Perlick's residential tech support runs through Parts Town — which explains a lot.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Here's the unusual thing we promised. <strong>Perlick outsources residential technical service to Parts Town.</strong> Not a rumour — the official technical-service contact address sits on the <code>partstown.com</code> domain, with a dedicated line on <strong>844-411-8050</strong>. Perlick's own customer service is <strong>800-558-5592</strong>. It also explains why the most complete public Perlick code documentation lives on Parts Town's site rather than Perlick's.</p>
        <p>There's no public authorized-servicer locator for the residential line, only a dealer locator; in practice warranty work routes through the dealer or distributor that sold the unit. So if you're in warranty — six years on that undercounter, remember — start there. That's the honest advice and it costs us the job.</p>
        <p>We're independent, not factory-authorized, and we say so before you ask. Where we're useful is out-of-warranty cabinets, second-owner units in houses that changed hands, bar builds where the original dealer has moved on, and the calls where you'd like someone this week. BHGS #A49573, and our name is on the invoice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-gray">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">06, coverage</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Perlick service across Greater Los Angeles.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Perlick lives where the home bar is the point: Beverly Hills, Bel Air, Brentwood, Pacific Palisades, Malibu, Manhattan Beach, plus the newer Hidden Hills and Calabasas builds and the Newport and Laguna outdoor kitchens. It's common for one house to have three of them — a wine reserve in the pantry, a beverage centre in the bar, a drawer set by the pool — bought at different times, from different series, and for only one to be the problem. Tell us which room.</p>
        <p>Call <a href={phoneHref}>{phone}</a> with the <strong>series and model off the plate</strong>, and the code if there is one. The series decides everything on this brand: whether codes exist at all, which warranty tier you're in, and whether the part is a stock item or a special order.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-number">07, FAQ</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Perlick residential repair, common questions.</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
